<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clement of Ohrid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 840–916</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Saint Clement of Ohrid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> (c. 840–916) was a Slavonic scholar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, one of the disciples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> in 885–886. Received by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-i-of-bulgaria">Boris I of Bulgaria</a>, he taught thousands of pupils in the region of Ohrid and became the first bishop to preach in the Slavonic language. He wrote many homilies and is traditionally linked with the spread of the Cyrillic alphabet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clement-of-ohrid.jpg" alt="Clement of Ohrid" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yane Bakreski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clement_of_Ohrid_icon_Zograf_Monastery_Athos.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The date and place of Clement's birth are unknown. Most scholars think he was a Slav from southern Macedonia, perhaps from the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, because his hagiographer Theophylact of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> says he knew the life of Methodius like no other; Theophylact calls him 'the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in the Bulgarian language'. He joined Methodius as a young man, following him to the monastery on Mysian Olympus, and took part in the mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> to Great Moravia. In 867 or 868 he was ordained a priest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by the bishops Formosus and Gauderic, together with two other disciples, Gorazd and Naum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the death of Methodius in 885, Clement and Gorazd led the struggle against the German clergy in Great Moravia. He spent some time in jail and was then expelled, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, then within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, in 885 or 886 with Naum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> and Angelarius. Clement and Naum were sent to the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a>, where Boris I commissioned them to instruct the future clergy in the Slavonic language. Since the adoption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 865 services had been conducted in Greek by clergy from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and Boris saw Old Slavonic as a way to preserve Bulgaria's independence.</p><p>Clement was sent to the south-west, to the region then called Kutmichevitsa, where he founded the Ohrid Literary School. For seven years, between 886 and 893, he taught some 3,500 disciples in Slavonic and the Glagolitic alphabet, and he and his co-workers translated Christian literature into Old Church Slavonic. Naum meanwhile worked on the school at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a>, which was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> in 893.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 893 Clement was ordained to the bishopric of Drembica, Velika. He died in 916 and was buried in his monastery of Saint Panteleimon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a>, and was soon canonised by the Bulgarian Orthodox Church. He was one of the most prolific writers in Old Church Slavonic. He is credited with the Panonic Hagiography of Saint Cyril and Saint Methodius, translated the Flower Triode, and is believed to be the author of the oldest service dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>. The invention of the Cyrillic alphabet is usually ascribed to him, although it was most likely developed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> school early in the 10th century. Sofia University, founded in 1888, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clement of Ohrid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When disciples of Cyril and Methodius, among them <b>Clement</b> and Naum, were expelled from Moravia in 885–886, Boris I received them and gave them the means to teach.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…carried it to Bulgaria, Croatia and Bohemia; at the Preslav Literary School Bulgarian disciples such as Naum and <b>Clement of Ohrid</b> created the Cyrillic alphabet, which became the script of the Bulgarian Empire and Kievan Rus', and Old Church Slavonic remains the liturgical language of several Orthodox churches.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…dissolved, the leading writers worked in Bulgaria at the end of the 9th and the start of the 10th century: <b>Clement of Ohrid</b>, who had also worked in Moravia and most likely founded the Ohrid school, Constantine of Preslav, Chernorizets Hrabar and John Exarch, with Naum of Preslav.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-church-slavonic">Old Church Slavonic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Byzantine Empire. Died in the Bulgarian Empire. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
